INTRO(2)

NAME
     intro - AZ/UX system calls

DESCRIPTION
     Programs call the kernel with trap #0: the call number in d0 and up
     to five arguments in d1-d5. The result comes back in d0; a value
     between -4095 and -1 is a negated error number (see <errno.h>). The
     C library wraps each call and sets errno.

     Number  Call            Number  Call
      0      exit            34      times
      1      fork            35      setgid
      2      read            36      getgid
      3      write           37      sigaction
      4      open            38      ioctl
      5      close           39      fcntl
      6      waitpid         40      setpgid
      7      link            41      umask
      8      unlink          42      dup2
      9      execve          43      getppid
     10      chdir           44      setsid
     11      time            45      getcwd
     12      mknod           46      getdents
     13      chmod           47      lstat
     14      chown           48      symlink
     15      brk             49      readlink
     16      stat            50      sleep_ms
     17      lseek           51      gettimeofday
     18      getpid          52      uname
     19      mount           53      sigreturn
     20      umount          54      sigprocmask
     21      setuid          55      getpgid
     22      getuid          56      reboot
     23      fstat           57      procinfo
     24      pause           58      sysinfo
     25      utime           59      alarm
     26      access          60      ftruncate
     27      sync            61      fsync
     28      kill            62      statfs
     29      rename          63      geteuid
     30      mkdir           64      getegid
     31      rmdir           65      settimeofday
     32      dup             66      mountinfo
     33      pipe

     The numbers are also in <sys/syscall.h>. Programs are loaded at
     0x40000000; the stack ends at 0x80000000 and grows on demand. At
     entry the stack holds argc, the argv pointers, a NULL, the envp
     pointers and a NULL.

SEE ALSO
     intro(3), as(1)
